From 6b7c10053ba790121299e1caf4bce02cd48492c5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vincenzo Grimaldi <vince.ceccarelli@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 14:55:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] 0043 replay the engine, not a model of it (5748 passed)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

tools/carry_backtest.py does not import CarryEngine. It never has. It
shares carry_costs.evaluate_entry with the trading program and reimplements
everything else: sizing, both-legs-or-neither, the negative-funding streak,
the delta band, the margin floor, the unwind, the fee accounting, the day's
entry allowance.

So EVERY NUMBER THIS REPOSITORY HAS PUBLISHED — +7.23 %/yr, the walk-forward
lines, the 0039 sweep, the maker-first comparison — came from a program that
is not the one that would trade, under a banner reading

    [x] rule_is_what_the_engine_runs

That box is true of the GATE. It was never true of the STATE MACHINE, and
the state machine is what decides whether an order is sent.

tools/carry_replay.py drives the real CarryEngine through history along
main.tick's exact path — take_snapshot, assert_fresh, on_candle — against a
ReplayBroker that answers every call the engine and the snapshot make.
Nothing about the decision is reimplemented.

THE VERDICT. The published number survives contact with the real class:

                  engine      simulator
  trades              79             79
  in market      90.98 %        90.98 %
  net         $29,766.40     $29,695.77
  annualised   +7.25 %/yr     +7.23 %/yr

and the $70.63 that remains is attributed to the cent: $66.64 is one trade
still open at the window end, for which the simulator charges an exit fee
and the engine has not sent that order; -$12.41 is the engine snapping to
Bybit's 0.001 lot step, which the simulator has no concept of; -$0.20 on
basis. The engine is right on both. Two tests pin the agreement and the
attribution; neither could exist before this commit.

TWO DEFECTS IN THE ENGINE, found by making it replayable.

D24 — the risk gate read the WALL clock. CarryRisk.gate_open and
record_entry dated entries from datetime.now(utc) while _open had
timestamp_ms in hand and never passed it. NOT a live bug: the two clocks
coincide live, and the fix is byte-identical in production. What it was is a
risk limit observable only in production — replayed, the first run opened
ONE trade in 4.11 years and refused 3,571 times with
ENTRY_LIMIT_REACHED_TODAY. A daily limit that agrees with the calendar only
in production is a daily limit nobody can test.

D25 — funding was booked on the ENTRY price. funding_collected +=
rate * perp.notional, and notional is filled_qty * avg_price, frozen at the
fill. The venue pays on the position's value at the SETTLEMENT mark. The
engine booked $29,371 where the same 79 trades earn $39,444 — a quarter of
the funding missing, because BTC rose while positions were held. It changes
no decision; it is the number a client is shown as "funding collected" and
that Phase E calls a bankable ledger. And the error's SIGN FOLLOWS THE
PRICE, which is intolerable in a book whose whole claim is that price
direction does not matter.

THREE DEFECTS IN THE HARNESS, found by diffing rather than by reading:
the overlay's basis dropped the long leg (a hedged pair became a naked short
and printed -32.76 %/yr for a position flat to price); funding was read
after the unwind, missing the print booked during it, and a book exits on
negative prints by construction, so the miss flattered it by $392; the exit
price came from the bar rather than the fill, so the closing leg's impact
vanished — exactly half of it, $882. Each surfaced as a number that would
not reconcile.

--bars replays a supplied settlement series (CSV or JSON) so history from
outside this repo can be tested against the engine itself. It refuses rather
than guesses: a missing column is named, a rate above 5% for one 8h print is
rejected as almost certainly bps rather than a fraction, and duplicate
stamps are refused rather than deduplicated.

Not modelled, and named in every report: latency, post-only fill
probability, the ticker's funding forecast, positionIM/positionMM (F4),
partial fills and rate limits. The first four are what the drill measures
and none of them closes with more code.

